    {#snippet en()}
        <h2>Fan websites</h2>
        <p><i>Bulánci</i> was released at a time when creating fan websites was becoming increasingly popular. These websites allowed fans to share their own creations and connect with other creators. In the case of <i>Bulánci</i>, the development of fan websites can be divided into two periods: the first, spanning approximately 2001–2006, and the second, covering 2006–2012. The first period was associated primarily with websites created using <b>free web hosting services</b> such as Webzdarma, while the second was largely shaped by the <b>Blog.cz platform</b>. During the first period, websites mainly served to distribute official content provided by the game's developers, which fans modified only slightly. The second period, by contrast, was characterized by the creation and distribution of fan-made maps. Alongside personal blogs, where creators published their own maps and highlighted the work of others, websites dedicated to collecting and aggregating fan-made maps also emerged.
        (<a href="https://dspace.cuni.cz/handle/20.500.11956/212593"><i>Playing, Sharing, Remembering</i></a>, Bachelor's thesis, Rudolf Jan Suchý, 2026)</p>
        <p>This overview contains <b>56 websites</b> archived and made accessible through the <a href="https://archive.org/">Internet Archive</a> project. Most of them are dedicated exclusively to <i>Bulánci</i>, but the selection also includes several personal websites and blogs that only touched on the game as a secondary topic. These demonstrate that distributing maps through a personal website was not limited to the most dedicated creators.</p>
